{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And I will give the rain of your land in its season.</b> In the hours suitable for sowing, such that you will find satisfaction in them. And so the Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״ונתתי גשמיכם בעתם״ — neither drunk (saturated) nor thirsty, but middling. When the rains are excessive, they blur the earth and it produces no fruit. Another interpretation: בעתם — on the nights of the fourth day (of the week) and on Sabbath nights. In the days of Rabbi Shimon ben Shetah they would fall on the nights of the fourth day and on Sabbath nights, until the wheat became as large as kidneys and the barley as olive pits and the lentils as gold dinars, and the Sages stored away a sample of them for future generations, to make known how much sin causes, as it is said (Jeremiah 5:25): ״עונותיכם הטו אלה וחטאתיכם מנעו הטוב מכם״. For thus we find in the days of Herod, that in the time when they were engaged in the building of the Temple, the rains would fall for them only at night; the next day the wind would blow, the clouds would scatter, and the sun would shine, and they would rise early and go out to their labor — to make known that the labor of Heaven was in their hands." "<b>The early rain and the latter rain.</b> Yoreh is the rain that falls after the sowing, in the first rainfall, which saturates the earth and the seeds. Or it is from the root הוראה (instruction), for it instructs the creatures to gather their fruits and plaster their roofs; or it is called so on account of its indicating a good year. Malkosh is the rain that falls last, close to the harvest, and it is called malkosh because it falls upon the stalks (מלילות) and upon the straw (קשין), and it is the late rain. And so the Targum renders (Genesis 30:42) העטופים — לקשיא; or it is called so because it מל (circumcises/removes) the hardness (קשיותן) of Israel — thus the Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted; the meaning is, when it is delayed, they pray concerning it and return in repentance." "<b>And you shall gather your grain and your wine.</b> The Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: But is it not written (Joshua 1:8) ״לא ימוש ספר התורה הזה מפיך״? Rather, conduct yourself with them in the way of the world (מנהג דרך ארץ): times for this and times for that. ״טוב אשר תאחוז בזה וגם מזה אל תנח ידך״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:34.232117645Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}